WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.140
Today.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:06.000
We welcome to the program Democratic Congresswoman
Barbara Lee, representing California's 12th

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.950
Congressional District, also a candidate for
the Senate seat currently held by Senator

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.950
Dianne Feinstein.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.650
Congresswoman, really a pleasure having you
on.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.379
I appreciate your time.

00:00:15.379 --> 00:00:16.410
Nice being with you, Dave.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.350
So, I mean, just to start with some of the
things that are that are going on right now,

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.210
yet again, we find ourselves in this position
of talking about the debt ceiling and are

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.289
we going to default and who's posturing politically
and for what different reasons.

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:32.610
Just as someone who's been through a number
of cycles of this.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.890
Is this even remotely interesting at this
point or is it just a feeling of exhaustion

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.340
that this has to happen time and time again?

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.690
No, it's not a feeling of exhaustion.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:48.180
I feel like that the Republicans, especially
the Republicans, have politicized this to

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.140
the point where it's confusing oftentimes
to the public when you incur debts.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.980
And remember, a large portion of these debts
were incurred under Donald Trump with his

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.230
tax giveaways to corporations and the mega-rich.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.590
When you incur your debt, you're obligated
to pay it.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.439
And when you buy a car, you pay your car note.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:12.190
If you default on your car note, they repossessed
the car.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.290
And then if you go to get another car, the
interest rates will go up if in fact you can

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.070
purchase another car.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:25.490
And so why they're politicizing this is primarily
to speak to their base and to the MAGA Republicans

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:33.440
who want to basically take away and cut Social
Security, cut veterans health, cut Medicare,

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.119
all of the cuts that they want to take place
around using people's lives really as hostage

00:01:40.119 --> 00:01:41.920
for the debt ceiling.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.950
And that's unacceptable.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.409
We must pay our bills.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:47.640
Then we talk about how we negotiate around
the budget.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.270
I'm a member of the Budget Committee and the
Appropriations Committee, and that's the process

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.380
that should be used at this point.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:02.360
Do you operate in Congress with the belief
that something like the debt ceiling debate,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.940
which feels so distant to the lives of so
many Americans who just they go to work, they

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.619
might bring kids to school, their living,
their lives?

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:15.800
Do you operate with the belief or assumption
that something like the debt ceiling debate

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:21.000
can be used both positively and negatively
electorally, or that it's the type of thing

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.000
that just turns people off?

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.170
Well, I think people need to understand the
dynamics when you talk about debt ceiling.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.430
We have to break it down.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.840
And I looked at Kevin McCarthy, the speaker,
the speaker's district.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.300
I mean, thousands of people will lose jobs.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.390
The House, the people in his district will
lose their veterans benefit thousands of people.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:48.470
Their retirement savings will be cut by at
least $20,000.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:52.620
So thousands of people in his district alone
are going to be impacted.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.420
When you look at child care, when you look
at early childhood education, when you look

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:02.730
at college education, young people need to
have college this debt free.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.390
It's going to cost a heck of a lot more for
young people to go to college if we defaulted

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.390
on the debt.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:13.069
So this has real impact on people's lives,
and that's why we're fighting so hard as Democrats.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:16.940
To get these Republicans to understand we
must pay our debts.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.209
We didn't have this issue under Donald Trump.

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:22.690
I mean, I think we raise the debt ceiling,
what, three times?

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.879
And that, you know, the Republicans were fighting.

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:28.280
And so this is a political issue.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:33.310
They're trying to cater to the base, to Marjorie
Taylor GREENE And they really don't care about

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.670
who's lives that they're hurting.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:40.830
But believe you me, Republican lives in Republican
districts and red states and blue states.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.470
People are going to be hurt in a big way if,
in fact, we don't do this.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:49.000
Congresswoman, with the benefit of hindsight,
we're now able to have quite, quite a precise

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.950
conversation about something like Afghanistan.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:58.680
If we go back to the moments decades ago when
you were the only member of Congress to vote

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.379
against the authorization of military force
in Afghanistan after 911.

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:11.420
And we now go to Joe Biden carrying out some
version of the plan Donald Trump wanted to

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.400
carry out to get us out of Afghanistan, which
while not perfect, I believe, was absolutely

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.639
the right decision.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:23.979
What can we now say in sum total about that
now that we have this these two decades going

00:04:23.979 --> 00:04:29.260
back to your vote against that authorization
all the way through to the exit, although

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.400
some contractors remain?

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.650
What's the what's the retrospective on Afghanistan?

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:40.080
Well, first of all, yes, I voted against the
authorization because it came three days after

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.380
the horrific attack on our country.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.090
First of all, thousands of people were died.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:51.980
Still, thousands of people are dealing with
the health impacts of what took place.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.259
And it was a 60 word authorization.

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:58.500
Three days after this horrific attack that
authorized not only President Bush, but any

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.500
subsequent president to go to war without
coming back to Congress.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:03.950
That's unconstitutional.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:09.680
I mean, we debate and authorize or not the
use of force for specific actions, not giving

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.110
the executive branch Congress is, which is
the people's voice, the authority.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.270
And there's no way I could vote for that.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.759
That was overly broad.

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:20.490
And it was it set the stage for perpetual
wars.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.380
Fast forward to today.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:29.009
We have used this administration, all administrations
since the Bush administration has used this,

00:05:29.009 --> 00:05:33.490
and I think 19 or 20 countries non-related
to nine, 1141 times even.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:38.539
And the Congressional Research Study provided
a declassified report.

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:42.699
It's been used for domestic surveillance in
our own country.

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:44.630
And so this was overly broad.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:50.160
As I said, it was all we needed to debate
and come up with an appropriate response.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.610
I'm not looking at the world through rose
colored glasses.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:57.730
I mean, I know we have to have some form of
a response, but you have to have a rational

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:03.460
response that's appropriate that three days
after, while people are mourning, grieving

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.580
and angry.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:10.639
And so now I'm trying to repeal this authorization,
and I've got bipartisan support to do that.

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:17.080
What the problem and the sticking point is
there are some who want to repeal and replace.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.440
But what do you replace it with?

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.199
The president has to come to Congress to have
an authorization to replace whatever you need

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:27.819
the authorization to use force for.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:32.199
So my repeal authorization stays in place
for eight months.

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:37.419
If the president determines he or she needs
an authorization to go to war, come back to

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:40.770
Congress, will debate it and provide it or
not.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:47.660
What's your view on the withdrawal from Afghanistan,
which, you know, it wasn't perfect, but I

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.419
believe it was the right decision.

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:54.380
There was there were a number of criticisms
made of Joe Biden's withdrawal.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.910
But when the former president, Donald Trump,
was initially asked what he would have done

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.639
differently, he wasn't really able to articulate
anything that he would have done differently.

00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:06.229
So it seems that maybe there would have been
some chaos no matter when it happened.

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:07.280
What's your sense of it?

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.060
Well, my sense of it is that it had to be
done.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.310
And yes, it was chaotic.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.310
It wasn't planned.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.849
But remember, this was set up by Donald Trump.

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:15.849
Yes.

00:07:15.849 --> 00:07:18.979
He made and so President Biden did the right
thing.

00:07:18.979 --> 00:07:27.500
It could have been done in a more organized
fashion where we could have I think then the

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:34.120
could have really provided a better safe passage
for those especially who protected U.S. troops

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.420
and who supported the U.S. in in Afghanistan,
the ousted population.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:46.030
But he had to do this because most military
officials will tell you we'd be there 20 more

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.849
years and then 20 more years.

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:51.289
There was no military solution in Afghanistan.

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:55.820
And so why would we stay there another 20
years?

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:57.830
Our young men and women served bravely.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:00.590
We lost a lot of troops.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:07.560
A lot of our young men and women have health
impacts that will be with them for the rest

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:08.669
of their lives.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:14.639
We have to make sure that they have the type
of medical care, employment and support that

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:19.919
they need, which of course the Republicans
are reticent to give them to support.

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:22.900
So, yes, President Biden did the right thing.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.699
No, it was done well.

00:08:24.699 --> 00:08:27.500
No, it was not done in an orderly way.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.849
But the historical context has to be looked
at.

00:08:29.849 --> 00:08:35.959
And what Donald Trump promised and how President
Biden had to execute right away, and that

00:08:35.959 --> 00:08:37.740
was what took place.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.209
Congresswoman, I want to talk a little bit
about the Senate race that you're involved

00:08:41.209 --> 00:08:42.209
in right now.

00:08:42.209 --> 00:08:44.089
This is a very interesting race.

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:49.500
We we've interviewed Adam Schiff, your colleague
and opponent in that race we hope to speak

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.500
to.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.310
Katie Porter, also your colleague, an opponent
in that race.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:55.480
You know, I get so many emails about that
race.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:01.459
And one of the big concerns that people have
is that there are three congressional seats

00:09:01.459 --> 00:09:09.970
here that are going to be vacated, which hopefully
would be filled by Democrats, all four one

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:15.870
Senate seat, which also, if Dianne Feinstein
were to resign, at least in the short term,

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:19.000
Gavin Newsom would get to appoint someone.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.529
Gavin Newsom has said if he had that opportunity,
he would appoint a black woman.

00:09:23.529 --> 00:09:26.120
Adam Schiff, Very nice guy, not a black woman.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:27.430
Katie Porter, same thing.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:30.420
So talk to me about the dynamics of this race.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:34.610
Are there discussions within the halls of
Congress about the fact that these.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:38.260
Three California House seats will be up for
grabs.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:39.490
Talk us through it a little bit.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.779
Well, I'm very focused on my race.

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:46.390
First of all, I have a very strong Democratic
district.

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:52.010
So I'm confident that we will elect a Democrat
in the 12th congressional District.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.279
And yes, I'm running.

00:09:53.279 --> 00:10:01.220
I was one of those that the governor had considered
once, once Vice President Kamala Harris resigned.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:07.140
And, of course, the governor appointed, who
was a good friend and doing a great job, Senator

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:08.870
Alex Padilla.

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:14.390
But the governor did say if he had an appointing
authority, that he would appoint an African

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:15.390
American woman.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.940
But let me tell you, I am running.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.060
And going back to when he said that there
were two African-American women at the top

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:23.060
of his list.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.610
One was at least we heard that was Mayor Karen
Bass and myself.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:33.290
But I decided when after I talked to Senator
Feinstein in December, that she was going

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:37.139
to make her announcement very soon whether
she was going to run or not run.

00:10:37.139 --> 00:10:38.139
And I waited.

00:10:38.139 --> 00:10:41.360
And so she made her announcement that she
was not going to run.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.410
That was in February.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.600
Of course, my opponents were running already.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:45.600
Yes.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:46.600
But I waited.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.360
I filed the next day after she announced she
was not running.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:50.360
And I'm running.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:55.540
And I think that my experience, my background,
my lived experiences, as well as my experiences

00:10:55.540 --> 00:11:02.260
as a negotiator and as a progressive black
woman and as someone who fights not only for

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:06.330
marginalized communities, but for everyone,
for the middle class, for working families,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:11.370
for people to have the quality of life that
they deserve, an economy that works for all

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.070
people who have not been seen.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:18.800
You know, here in California, we have 20,
20% of our population is one paycheck away

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:19.800
from poverty.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.780
No one in the Senate is fighting for the poor
and low income people and working families.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.320
You don't hear that debate.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:28.029
You don't hear the debate around climate change.

00:11:28.029 --> 00:11:35.029
That and addressing climate the climate crisis
with environmental justice as part of that

00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:39.160
in terms of what we do to mitigate the harm
of low income communities.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:45.459
I am running in this race on a platform to
talk about inequality in housing and in my

00:11:45.459 --> 00:11:46.570
shared experiences.

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:49.860
I had an abortion when I was 15 years old.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.880
I co-chair the pro-choice caucus.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:53.980
I fought to end the Hyde Amendment.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:57.079
I had I talked about what happened during
my abortion.

00:11:57.079 --> 00:12:04.480
Not only did I do that well, I did that because
finally with the Texas decision, I felt like

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.810
the public should know that there are members
of Congress who have these shared experiences.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:16.010
And I am fighting now to make sure that we
codify abortion care into our law.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:20.410
And so I have many, many experiences as a
small business owner or former small business

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.839
owner as a person start a community mental
health center.

00:12:23.839 --> 00:12:28.959
My background is mental health and my MSW
in psychiatric social work and no one else.

00:12:28.959 --> 00:12:32.120
A big issue here in California throughout
the country.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:36.810
And so I think my experience is my ability
to negotiate my standing strong, sometimes

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.760
having to take on my party when I think they're
wrong.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:45.320
And I have courage enough to do that because
this is who I am.

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:47.200
And of course, there are gaps in the Senate.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:48.900
There are no black women in the Senate.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:52.520
That perspective, the experience.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.050
Representation matters, David.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.070
Representation matters.

00:12:55.070 --> 00:13:01.019
It seems there are basically three storylines
that are playing the largest role in what

00:13:01.019 --> 00:13:04.200
are still the relatively early days of this
campaign.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.339
One storyline is identity as you talk about.

00:13:07.339 --> 00:13:12.680
A third storyline is who is the true or most
progressive.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:18.950
And the third storyline is experience versus
age and sort of like what the voters are looking

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:19.950
for.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:24.079
It seems as though this could get a little
bit awkward in the sense that I know you get

00:13:24.079 --> 00:13:26.560
along well with your colleagues from California.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.149
Do you have some conversation where everybody
says, hey, let's keep this clean?

00:13:31.149 --> 00:13:34.029
Or It strikes me as the type of thing that
could become awkward?

00:13:34.029 --> 00:13:36.720
Well, I don't think it's going to be all of
it.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.000
I mean, it's a campaign, right?

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:40.000
Yes.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.750
But believe you me, I know for a fact myself,
people don't like campaigns where you're hitting

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:46.750
below the belt.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:51.670
And I was always and I was mentored by Congressman
Ron Dellums.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.240
I worked for Ron for 11 years.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:56.100
He was the first black chair of the Armed
Services Committee.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:01.160
And Ron always told me and everyone on his
staff, you debate the policy, you bring great

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:05.899
experiences, you debate what your your historical
context and how you're going to use that for

00:14:05.899 --> 00:14:07.779
the future to make lives better.

00:14:07.779 --> 00:14:08.959
You don't hit below the belt.

00:14:08.959 --> 00:14:09.959
You don't go personal.

00:14:09.959 --> 00:14:10.959
You don't.

00:14:10.959 --> 00:14:12.410
So, no, I don't do that.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:14.130
I mean, I'm going to defend myself.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:15.130
Yes.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:17.380
If their lives told about me, I mean, come
on.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:19.870
But I don't think it's an awkward campaign.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.300
It's a this is a democracy.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:25.370
People put out their record, who they are,
what they stand for, what they've done and

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:26.734
what they're going to do in the future.

00:14:26.734 --> 00:14:30.160
And then people vote based on who they think
will best represent them.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:33.579
Well, I'll tell you, a campaign based on policy
sound.

00:14:33.579 --> 00:14:38.519
So refreshing given the context in which the
Republican Party has abandoned, even pretending

00:14:38.519 --> 00:14:39.829
to talk about policy.

00:14:39.829 --> 00:14:43.089
So that, I think, would be welcomed by many,
many voters.

00:14:43.089 --> 00:14:44.830
We wish you the best in the campaign.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:49.600
We've been speaking with Congresswoman Barbara
Lee, representing California's 12th Congressional

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:50.600
District.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:51.970
I really appreciate your time and insights
